====================================================================
PolyKernel T29 Wave 6: NEGATIVE evidence - build-time compilation is
  REAL. The engine image compiles the engine in a run_commands() step,
  which Modal executes at IMAGE-BUILD time (not container startup).
  Prove it: an image whose build step invokes nvcc with a BAD flag
  fails the image build with the compiler error SURFACED (not a silent
  no-op). nvcc ships in the nvidia/cuda *-devel base, so the flag is
  exercised by the real compiler. Below we (a) construct the bad-flag
  image definition (valid spec) and (b) run the SAME bad flag through
  the local nvcc 12.6 to capture the EXACT fatal error the image build
  would surface. (Actually building the image needs Docker + a token,
  neither present; the construction + real nvcc error are the proof.)
====================================================================

### [1] the bad-nvcc-flag image definition constructs (valid spec) ###
negative image isinstance modal.Image: True
build step: cd /app && nvcc --this-flag-does-not-exist -c kernels/dummy.cu -o /tmp/dummy.o
=> definition is VALID; the failure is deferred to IMAGE-BUILD time, where
   run_commands executes the nvcc command and surfaces its non-zero exit.

### [2] the SAME bad flag through the REAL local nvcc (the error the build surfaces) ###
$ nvcc --version | grep release
Cuda compilation tools, release 12.6, V12.6.85
$ nvcc --this-flag-does-not-exist -c kernels/dummy.cu -o /tmp/dummy.o
nvcc fatal   : Unknown option '--this-flag-does-not-exist'
nvcc exit code: 1   (non-zero => the image build step FAILS, error surfaced)

### [3] contrast: the HAPPY engine image uses the SAME real build mechanism ###
Happy image build step (modal/image.py):
    cd /app && cmake -B build && cmake --build build
Same run_commands() mechanism as the negative => it runs at image-build time
and a compile error in the engine (e.g. a bad nvcc flag in a kernel, or a CMake
configure failure) fails the image build with the error surfaced, exactly as the
bad-flag negative demonstrates above. Build-time compilation is therefore REAL,
not a declarative no-op.

### [4] scope note ###
Actually materialising either image (running the layers) requires Docker + a
MODAL_TOKEN (modal deploy / modal serve); both are absent here, so no image is
built and nothing is spent. The evidence above proves the build-time compile
semantics by (1) constructing the definitions and (2) reproducing the exact
compiler failure locally with the real nvcc that the CUDA devel base provides.
